<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ws-graphs" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Graphs</title>

  <worksheet xml:id="ws-graphs-hamiltonian">
    <title>Hamiltonian or Not?</title>
    <page>
      <figure>
        <caption>Find a hamiltonian cycle in each graph or explain why there is not one</caption>
      <sbsgroup >
      <sidebyside widths="30% 40% 30%">
        <figure>
          <caption><m>\bfG_1</m></caption>
    <image>
      <shortdescription>A graph</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="img-hamgraph1">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,300)" margins="5">
          <definition>graph={1:[4,5],2:[3,6,9],3:[7,10],4:[7,8,10],5:[6,7],6:[8],8:[9],9:[10]}</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
            <network graph="graph" scale="0.8"
                    node-fill="#fcf" node-stroke="black"
                    seed="1" labels="yes">
            </network>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </figure>
    <figure>
          <caption><m>\bfG_2</m></caption>
    <image>
      <shortdescription>A graph</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="img-hamgraph2">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
          <definition>graph={1:[2,10],2:[5,9,4,7],3:[7,8,9],4:[10],5:[6],6:[8,9]}</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
            <network graph="graph" scale="0.8"
                    node-fill="#fcf" node-stroke="black"
                    layout="circular" labels="yes">
            </network>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </figure>
    <figure>
          <caption><m>\bfG_3</m></caption>
    <image>
      <shortdescription>A graph</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="img-hamgraph3">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,300)" margins="5">
          <definition>graph={1:[5,7],2:[3,5,6,7],3:[8,10],4:[7,9,10],5:[6],8:[9],9:[10]}</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
            <network graph="graph" scale="0.8"
                    node-fill="#fcf" node-stroke="black"
                    layout="spring" labels="yes">
            </network>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </figure>
  </sidebyside>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
  <figure>
          <caption><m>\bfG_4</m></caption>
    <image>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="petersen-graph">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="15">
          <definition>N=5</definition>
          <definition>g(t)=(0.5*cos(2*pi*t/N), 0.5*sin(2*pi*t/N))</definition>
          <definition>f(t)=(cos(2*pi*t/N), sin(2*pi*t/N))</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
            <network  scale="0.8"
              node-fill="#fcf" node-stroke="black"
              seed="1" labels="yes" 
              tactile-node-size="40">
              <node at="0" p="f(0)" edges="[1,4,5]"/>
              <node at="1" p="f(1)" edges="[2,6]"/>
              <node at="2" p="f(2)" edges="[3,7]"/>
              <node at="3" p="f(3)" edges="[4,8]"/>
              <node at="4" p="f(4)" edges="[9]"/>
              <node at="5" p="g(0)" edges="[7,8]"/>
              <node at="6" p="g(1)" edges="[8,9]"/>
              <node at="7" p="g(2)" edges="[9]"/>
              <node at="8" p="g(3)" edges="[]"/>
              <node at="9" p="g(4)" edges="[]"/>
            </network>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </figure>
  <figure>
          <caption><m>\bfG_5</m></caption>
    <image>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="petersen-plus-e">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="15">
          <definition>N=5</definition>
          <definition>g(t)=(0.5*cos(2*pi*t/N), 0.5*sin(2*pi*t/N))</definition>
          <definition>f(t)=(cos(2*pi*t/N), sin(2*pi*t/N))</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
            <network  scale="0.8"
              node-fill="#fcf" node-stroke="black"
              seed="1" labels="yes" 
              tactile-node-size="40">
              <node at="0" p="f(0)" edges="[1,4,5]"/>
              <node at="1" p="f(1)" edges="[2,6]"/>
              <node at="2" p="f(2)" edges="[3,7]"/>
              <node at="3" p="f(3)" edges="[4,8]"/>
              <node at="4" p="f(4)" edges="[9]"/>
              <node at="5" p="g(0)" edges="[7,8]"/>
              <node at="6" p="g(1)" edges="[8,9]"/>
              <node at="7" p="g(2)" edges="[9,8]"/>
              <node at="8" p="g(3)" edges="[]"/>
              <node at="9" p="g(4)" edges="[]"/>
            </network>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>   
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </sbsgroup>
</figure>
  </page>
  <page component="ham-not-soln">
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{1}</m> is hamiltonian.
            One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>1,5,7,3,2,6,8,9,10,4</m>.
            (There are others.)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{2}</m> is not hamiltonian.
            One reason for this is that removing the vertex <m>2</m> leaves a disconnected graph.
            Put another way, <m>1,10,4,2</m> forms a cycle, and the only way to get from the other vertices onto that cycle is via vertex <m>2</m>.
            Once you're onto it, there's no way off again.
            Thus, there's no way to construct a cycle containing all <m>10</m> vertices.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{3}</m> is hamiltonian.
            One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>1,5,6,2,3,8,9,10,4,7</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{4}</m> is not hamiltonian.
            This is a nontrivial fact.
            For those interested in a complete proof, see the end of this document.
            (You will not be tested on the proof, so it's purely optional reading.)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{5}</m> (formed by adding one edge to the Petersen graph <m>\bfG_{1}</m>) is hamiltonian.
            One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>10,7,1,5,8,4,6,3,2,9</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>


    <proof>
      <title>Proof the Petersen Graph is not hamiltonian</title>

      <p>
        Consider what a hamiltonian cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m> would look like.
        The graph has <m>15</m> edges, and a hamiltonian cycle would use <m>10</m> of them.
        Thus, you'd have the cycle and five leftover edges that would connect vertices across the cycle (<q>chords</q>).
        How would these edges fit along the cycle? If an edge connected a vertex to a vertex two steps down the cycle (for example, if the cycle contains the sequence <m>a,b,c</m> of vertices and there's an edge from <m>a</m> to <m>c</m>), then there would be a triangle (a cyle on three vertices) in the graph.
        There is no such cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
        If a chord connects a vertex to another vertex at distance three along the hamiltonian cycle, then there is a cycle on four vertices.
        However, there is no such cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now suppose that a chord connects a vertex <m>v_{0}</m> to another vertex <m>v_{1}</m> at distance four along the hamiltonian cycle.
        Let <m>v_{0}'</m> be the vertex opposite <m>v_{0}</m> along the hamiltonian cycle.
        There is also a chord incident with <m>v_{0}'</m>, connecting it across the hamiltonian cycle to a vertex <m>v_{1}'</m>.
        We've already explained that the distance from <m>v_{0}'</m> to <m>v_{1}'</m> along the hamiltonian cycle cannot be less than four, and it cannot be five since that would give <m>v_{0}'</m> degree four.
        Thus the distance along the hamiltonian cycle from <m>v_{0}'</m> to <m>v_{1}'</m> must be four.
        Now <m>v_{0},v_{1},v_{1}',v_{0}'</m> is a cycle of length four (draw a picture to convince yourself), which we don't have in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
        Finally, the only option that remains is that the chords all connect vertices that are exactly opposite each other on the hamiltonian cycle.
        However, then if <m>v_{0}</m> and <m>v_{1}</m> are adjacent along the hamiltonian cycle and <m>v_{0}'</m> and <m>v_{1}'</m> are the vertices opposite them, <m>v_{0},v_{1},v_{1}',v_{0}'</m> is a cycle of length four.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </page>
  </worksheet>

</chapter>